CH = dict(num=12, title="How Nature Works in Harmony", theme="Ecosystems, food chains, balance",
 intro="When forests shrink and waterholes dry, elephants wander into farms — one change leads to another. This chapter shows how living and non-living parts of a **habitat** form an **ecosystem**, how organisms are linked through **food chains and food webs**, how **decomposers** recycle nutrients, how **interactions keep nature balanced**, and how human activity, especially farming, can disturb that balance.",
 big_questions=["How might loss of forest cover and changed rainfall lead elephants into farms and villages?", "Can the Earth thrive without humans? Can humans survive without the Earth?", "Can human actions cause natural disasters?"],
 blocks=[
 ("p", "**Elephant example:** in Odisha, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Assam and Chhattisgarh, elephants enter farms and villages when vegetation is scarce and waterholes dry up, seeking bananas and sugarcane. Climate change in rainfall/temperature and felling of trees for roads and buildings shrink forests; with **habitat loss** animals move into human areas, causing crop damage and harm. **Elephant corridors** connect forest habitats so animals can move safely without conflict."),
 ("h2", "12.1 How Do We Experience and Interpret Our Surroundings?"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **habitat** is the place where an organism lives (even tree bark). It has **biotic components** (living: plants, animals, microorganisms) and **abiotic components** (non-living: air, sunlight, water, temperature, soil)."),
 ("table", ["Habitat", "Biotic components", "Abiotic components"], [
   ["Pond", "Fish, frogs, turtles, snakes, dragonflies, mosquitoes, snails, ducks, algae, diatoms, duckweed, lotus", "Water, sunlight, oxygen in water, soil, temperature"],
   ["Forest", "Grass, trees, birds, deer, microbes", "Soil, air, sunlight, water, temperature"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Activity 12.1:** list living and non-living things of two habitats (pond, forest, farm, large tree) in groups with a teacher. Both have living and non-living parts; the types differ.",
         "A pond gives fish food, oxygen, shelter and space: food from small plants and animals (biotic), oxygen from water (abiotic).",
         "Different organisms share resources differently — a nocturnal snake and a day-active rodent live in the same forest under different conditions, so they **coexist**."]),
 ("h2", "12.2 Who All Live Together in Nature?"),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning", "Example"], [
   ["**Population**", "Group of the **same kind** of organisms living in a habitat at a given time", "All the fish of one kind in a pond; 20 plants of one kind in a 1 m × 1 m patch"],
   ["**Community**", "All the **different populations** (plants, animals, microorganisms) sharing a habitat", "Fish, frogs, algae, insects of a pond"],
   ["**Ecosystem**", "Community + abiotic components interacting", "Pond, forest, farm"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Activity 12.2:** mark a 1 m × 1 m area of the school garden, identify four organisms, count them (population) and pool group data.",
         "If all organisms were the same they would need the same food, water and space, causing **competition and scarcity**.",
         "**Pollination:** flower has sepals, petals, **carpels (female)** and **stamens (male, release pollen)**. Wind, water, insects, bats and birds carry pollen — essential for fruits and seeds."]),
 ("h2", "12.3 Does Every Organism in a Community Matter?"),
 ("p", "**Activity 12.3 — Pond study:** Pond A (with fish) had many flowering plants nearby; Pond B (no fish) had fewer. Fish eat **dragonfly larvae**, so Pond A had fewer dragonflies; dragonflies eat flies, bees and butterflies, so Pond A had **more pollinators**, hence more pollination and **more seeds** in nearby flowers."),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "This shows **direct and indirect effects**: every organism matters, and biotic (fish, dragonflies, pollinators, plants) and abiotic (temperature, water, nutrients) parts affect one another. Overfishing could upset this balance."),
 ("h2", "12.4 Interactions Among Organisms and Their Surroundings"),
 ("table", ["Criterion", "Kind of interaction", "Example"], [
   ["1", "Between **biotic and abiotic** components", "Earthworms live in moist soil; a fish lays eggs in water; microbes in pond water"],
   ["2", "Between **two abiotic** components", "Sunlight raises temperature and evaporates water fast; air currents make waves"],
   ["3", "Among **biotic** components", "Frog eats insects; water snake eats fish; frog and fish compete for larvae"]]),
 ("p", "Biotic and abiotic components interacting form an **ecosystem**. Ecosystems can be large or small and may overlap:"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Examples"], [
   ["**Aquatic**", "Ponds, rivers, lakes, seas, oceans"],
   ["**Terrestrial**", "Forests, grasslands, deserts, farms, large trees (banyan, mango, pilkhan)"],
   ["Human-made", "Farmland, fish ponds, parks"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Biotic depend on abiotic:** sunlight, CO<sub>2</sub> and water for making food; soil gives nutrients and support; air gives oxygen for respiration; water is essential for all.",
         "**Abiotic are influenced by biotic:** plants release oxygen, roots hold soil and prevent erosion, plants retain soil moisture and help cool the atmosphere."]),
 ("h3", "Feeding habits"),
 ("table", ["Group", "Meaning", "Examples"], [
   ["**Producers (autotrophs)**", "Make own food by photosynthesis (auto = self, troph = food)", "Green plants, trees, grass"],
   ["**Consumers (heterotrophs)**", "Depend on other organisms for food (hetero = other)", "Animals"],
   ["Herbivores", "Eat only plants", "Deer, hare"],
   ["Carnivores", "Eat only animals", "Leopard, tiger, vulture"],
   ["Omnivores", "Eat plants and animals", "Crow, fox, mouse"],
   ["**Decomposers (saprotrophs)**", "Break down dead matter (sapro = rotten)", "Fungi (mushroom), bacteria"]]),
 ("h2", "12.5 Who Eats Whom?"),
 ("ul", ["A **food chain** is a simple linear sequence showing ‘who eats whom’: *Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake → Eagle*; the arrow points to the eater.",
         "Each step is a **trophic level**: producers (1st), herbivores (2nd), small carnivores such as frogs (3rd), large carnivores such as tigers or vultures (next).",
         "**Pyramid (Activity 12.7):** numbers are highest at the base (millet) and lowest at the top (eagle), giving a pyramid shape.",
         "Organisms are eaten by two or more kinds of animals, so chains interlink into a network — a **food web** (Activity 12.8: hawk, snake, fox, owl, hare, mouse, frog, bird, grasshopper, grasses)."]),
 ("h2", "12.6 What Happens to Waste in Nature?"),
 ("ul", ["Dead plants/animals and waste are broken down by fungi and bacteria (mushrooms grow on dead matter in rains; beetles and flies on dung) — **decomposition**.",
         "Complex substances become simple ones and **nutrients return to the soil** for plants. Decomposers (saprotrophs) therefore **recycle nutrients** — in nature nothing is wasted.",
         "**Migratory birds** (e.g. Demoiselle cranes at Khichan, Jodhpur, in winter) move to avoid harsh climate and find food, act as pollinators and seed dispersers, link habitats and eat insect pests, helping farmers."]),
 ("h2", "12.7 How Does One Change Lead to Another?"),
 ("steps", [
   ("Chain reaction in a pond", "Pollution kills pond plants → **less oxygen** → fish die → fewer consumers → insects increase → spread to farms → farmers use more **pesticides** → further environmental damage."),
   ("Activity 12.9 — Indian bullfrog", "In the 1980s India exported large numbers of **frog legs**; frog populations fell, frogs’ insect prey (agricultural pests) rose, farmers used more synthetic pesticides that harmed soil, water and health. The government **banned frog-leg export**.")]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "An ecosystem is in **balance** when interactions keep populations and resources stable. This balance is **dynamic, not fixed** and can be upset by natural or human-made changes."),
 ("h2", "12.8 How Do Interactions Maintain Balance?"),
 ("p", "Organisms **compete** for food, water, space or sunlight; competition limits population size and prevents one species from multiplying too much. Other relationships:"),
 ("table", ["Relationship", "Effect", "Example"], [
   ["**Mutualism**", "Both benefit", "Honeybee gets nectar, flower gets pollinated"],
   ["**Commensalism**", "One benefits, the other unaffected", "Orchid on a tree gets support; the tree branch is unaffected"],
   ["**Parasitism**", "One benefits, the other harmed", "Tick feeds on dog’s blood; dog gets skin irritation"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**A.J.T. Johnsingh**, wildlife biologist, pioneered modern tracking and showed (in Bandipur) that predators like tigers and leopards depend on a healthy prey population such as deer and wild boar."),
 ("h2", "12.9 Benefits of an Ecosystem"),
 ("ul", ["Forests give fresh air, fertile soil, food, fibre, timber and medicines; aquatic ecosystems give water and food; ecosystems also have aesthetic and recreational value and regulate climate.",
         "**Sundarbans:** world’s largest mangrove forest where Ganga and Brahmaputra meet (India–Bangladesh); slows winds and waves in storms and floods, absorbs CO<sub>2</sub> and gives oxygen; **UNESCO World Heritage Site (1987)**.",
         "**Threats:** cutting mangroves for fuelwood and farming, illegal hunting, overuse, industrial waste and sewage. Mangroves also protected Selvam’s village in the 2004 tsunami by reducing wave force.",
         "General threats: deforestation, overuse of resources, **invasive species**, unsustainable land use, pollution, climate change.",
         "**Protected areas:** national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, biosphere reserves, community conserved areas — e.g. Jim Corbett (Uttarakhand), Manas (Assam), Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, Chilika Lake (Odisha), Eaglenest (Arunachal Pradesh), Hemis (Leh), Keibul Lamjao (Manipur), Pirotan Island Marine (Gujarat)."]),
 ("h3", "12.9.1 Human-made ecosystems"),
 ("p", "Fish ponds, farms and parks meet human needs; if well designed they reduce pollution, support biodiversity and give recreation, but unlike natural ecosystems they **need human care and management**."),
 ("h3", "12.9.2 Healthy ecosystems and farming"),
 ("ul", ["Between 1950 and 1965 India faced a food crisis; tractors, machines, **synthetic fertilisers and pesticides** raised production — the **Green Revolution**.",
         "These methods are now considered unsustainable: **overuse of chemicals, excessive groundwater extraction, monoculture** (growing one crop repeatedly).",
         "Overusing fertilisers kills useful soil microorganisms and reduces **humus**, so soil erodes; pesticides kill natural predators (e.g. beetles that eat pests) so pests increase; pests become **resistant**; heavy irrigation and ploughing disturb earthworms and snails; monoculture reduces biodiversity and pollinators.",
         "Solution: **organic and natural farming**, minimal interference, crop variety, recycling farm waste.",
         "**Heritage:** the *Vrikshayurveda* advocates soil nourishment with organic manure such as **Kunapa Jala**, a fermented liquid fertiliser from animal and plant waste."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Questions with Model Answers"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Define habitat, biotic and abiotic components.", "Habitat: place where an organism lives. Biotic: living components; abiotic: non-living such as air, water, soil, sunlight."),
  ("Distinguish population, community and ecosystem.", "Population: same kind of organisms; community: all populations in a habitat; ecosystem: community plus abiotic factors interacting."),
  ("Name the two types of ecosystems with examples.", "Aquatic (pond, river, lake, sea) and terrestrial (forest, grassland, farm)."),
  ("Who are producers, consumers and decomposers?", "Producers make food by photosynthesis; consumers eat plants/animals; decomposers (bacteria, fungi) break down dead matter."),
  ("What is a food chain and a food web?", "A food chain is a linear ‘who eats whom’ sequence; food webs are interlinked chains."),
  ("What are trophic levels?", "Positions of organisms in a food chain."),
  ("Why are decomposers essential?", "They recycle nutrients to the soil; otherwise dead matter piles up and plants lack nutrients."),
  ("Distinguish mutualism, commensalism and parasitism with examples.", "Both benefit (bee–flower); one benefits other unaffected (orchid on tree); one benefits other harmed (tick on dog)."),
  ("How did frog-leg export affect farming?", "Fewer frogs meant more insect pests, more pesticide use and environmental harm."),
  ("Why are mangroves important?", "They slow winds and waves in storms, absorb CO2 and shelter wildlife."),
  ("Why is monoculture harmful?", "It reduces biodiversity and pollinators and degrades soil."),
  ("What happens to grasshoppers and snakes if frogs disappear?", "Grasshoppers increase (no predator) and snakes decrease (less food).")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["A **habitat** has **biotic** and **abiotic** components; their interaction forms an **ecosystem** (aquatic or terrestrial).",
  "**Population → community → ecosystem** go from smaller to larger.",
  "**Producers** make food, **consumers** eat plants/animals, **decomposers** recycle nutrients.",
  "**Food chains** link into **food webs**; positions are **trophic levels**.",
  "**Mutualism, commensalism, parasitism** and **competition** keep balance; small changes can cascade.",
  "Human activities (deforestation, pollution, monoculture, overuse of chemicals) threaten ecosystems; **protected areas** and sustainable farming conserve them."])
